<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernesto Cardenal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernesto Cardenal was a Nicaraguan poet and Catholic priest, a leading figure of liberation theology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. He founded a Christian community on the Solentiname islands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nicaragua">Lake Nicaragua</a> and supported the Sandinista revolution, serving as minister of culture from 1979 to 1987; Pope John Paul II publicly rebuked him in 1983. His poetry, including Hora 0 and the Salmos, made him one of the best-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American poets of his time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernesto-cardenal.jpg" alt="Ernesto Cardenal" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Mejía peralta from Managua, Nicaragua · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidaridad_Ernesto_Cardenal_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
